fix(mcp): give a merge commit its first-parent patch id (#229)

`diff-tree -p --root` prints nothing for a commit with more than one parent,
so Phase C could not identify the change in an ordinary merge and the
invariant fired on it. Measured on a real merge signed against a live
deployment: the --root diff is 0 bytes and yields no patch id at all, while
the first-parent diff is 211 bytes and yields 0e175db78acb3622f29f3931edfdc7fcf5cb1a8d
-- which is the patch id the merged branch's own commit already had.

First parent is what Phase A was already asking. `StagedPatchID` runs
`git diff --cached`, which compares the index against HEAD, and when a merge
is committed HEAD is the first parent; the two phases now agree by
construction rather than by coincidence. `--cc` is the obvious reach and is
not the answer: on a merge that resolved no conflicts it emits the commit sha
and no patch, so the empty-diff refusal would only move one step later. The
non-merge invocation is unchanged byte for byte, because every patch id
already in the ledger was computed from it.

The signer's refusal no longer claims a rollback that never happens. A
failure after Phase B leaves the commit at HEAD carrying its identity
trailers, with Rekor holding its entry and only the ledger record missing.
The script now asks git which of the two states it is in: it names the
commit, says what is missing, and sends the operator to the reconciler
instead of printing "nothing was committed" -- the sentence that makes an
operator commit a second time or reset a commit Rekor already holds.

Driven by four tests, each observed failing first. MCP-067 refused a real
merge with "no change to identify". MCP-068 panicked on a root commit when
the parent lookup was made to report a parent it did not have. MCP-069 named
diff-tree instead of rev-list when the lookup swallowed its own failure, and
returned a patch id when the lookup ignored the configured git. MCP-070 read
back "nothing was committed" over a commit that was at HEAD.

